  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Introduced in "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Dragonlance: Shadow of the Dragon Queen",
              "href": "https://www.amazon.com/Dragonlance-Shadow-Dungeons-Dragons-Adventure/dp/0786968281?crid=L748UULXKMQN&keywords=shadow+of+the+dragon+queen&qid=1669671951&sprefix=shadow+of+the+dragon%2Caps%2C418&sr=8-1&linkCode=ll1&tag=rpgbot-20&linkId=aeea11fa20cb55ac9953fbcafe0b78d7&language=en_US&ref_=as_li_ss_tl"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " ("
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "affiliate link",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/about/faq#what-are-affiliate-links-and-why-do-you-use-them"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "), the Kender is a race unique to the Dragonlance world of Krynn. They have a storied history in the long-running Dragonlance novel series, and they hold a controversial place in the D&D community, beloved by Dragonlance fans and often disliked by other players only tangentially familiar with the Kender’s lore."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Historically, Kender have been portrayed unfavorably because of their loose\n\tconcept of private property. Within an adventuring party, this could encourage\n\tthe worst rogue impulses, including theft from fellow party members with the\n\texcuse of “it’s what my character would do.” That flavor has been scrubbed\n\taway in 5e’s interpretation, and instead Kender lean more into being\n\tinquisitive, curious explorers and collectors rather than light-fingered\n\tmenaces."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, the Kender feels very similar to the Halfling; Small size and a natural protection against fear evocative of the Halfling’s Brave trait. The Kender’s signature racial trait is Taunt, which allows them to debuff a creature as a Bonus Action, making it difficult for the target to attack other creatures."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Taunt is satisfying in a number of ways, but it does mean that the Kender\n\tfavors builds with higher mental ability scores over strictly martial builds.\n\tThis is unfortunate, because a taunt mechanic often makes it much easier to be\n\tan effective Defender. Builds which already lean heavily on their Bonus Action\n\tmay find it difficult to fit Taunt into their tactics, but builds which don’t\n\twill find Taunt a wonderful addition to their tactical options."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
