    {
      "sectionId": "section-disclaimer",
      "heading": "Understanding RPGBOT’s Rating System",
      "sourceAnchor": "disclaimer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "RPGBOT uses a color coding scheme which is simple to understand and easy to read at a glance. This system is designed to help players make informed decisions for their dnd character builds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-2",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Red"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Bad, useless options, or options which\n\t\tare extremely situational. Nearly never useful."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Orange"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": OK options, or useful options\n\t\tthat only apply in rare circumstances. Useful sometimes."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Green"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Good options. Useful often."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Blue"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Fantastic options, often essential\n\t\tto the function of your character. Useful very frequently."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Classes",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Artificers have numerous uses for their Bonus Action, which makes Taunt\n\tdifficult to use. A stout halfling will frequently be a better choice with a\n\tsimilar feel."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Barbarians almost universally dump their mental ability scores, so Taunt will\n\tusually be unreliable. Many barbarians also lean heavily on their Bonus\n\tAction."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "While bards have many ways to use their Bonus Action, they’re all limited use (Bardic Inspiration, spells, etc.), so turns in which you’re not using your Bonus Action occur often enough that Taunt is appealing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A Kender lore bard can max out their action economy by using Vicious Mockery as an Action, Taunt as their Bonus Action, and Cutting Words as their Reaction, and spend all of their actions for a round doing nothing except insulting people. This isn’t abnormally powerful, but it’s fun and it can shut down one creature’s attacks for a turn quite a bit."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Clerics have many excellent uses for their Bonus Action, including spells\n\tlike Healing Word, Shield of Faith, and Spiritual Weapon. However, those all\n\tcost expensive and limited spell slots, and often can’t be used when you’re\n\tcasting another spell on the same turn. As a whole, this means that you’ll\n\thave occasional turns where your Bonus Action is unused, but not many beyond\n\tlow levels."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most Druids have few consistent uses for their Bonus Action, so Taunt is easy to fit into your tactics. Taunt does require speech, unfortunately, so you won’t be able to use it while using Wild Shape. This makes Circle of the Land and "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Circle of Spores",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/classes/druid/subclasses/circle-of-spores/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " particularly good choices."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "While most fighters don’t lean heavily on their Bonus Action, they also\n\tgenerally don’t have mental stats good enough to make Taunt reliable. If you\n\tcan spare a bit of Wisdom to support it (Charisma for Purple Dragon Knights,\n\tIntelligence for Eldritch Knights), you might do alright."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Monks have too much competition for their Bonus Action to fit Taunt into\n\ttheir tactics easily, though of every martial class they’re among the most\n\tlikely to have mental stats to support it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Paladins often have Charisma close to that of full spellcasters (if not outright matching a full caster), and aside from a handful of spells, most paladins have few uses for their Bonus Actions, making Taunt easy to fit into your tactics. It does compete for space with Compelled Duel, but Taunt doesn’t require Concentration and doesn’t eat a spell slot, so it adds to your existing resources without directly competing with them. Unfortunately, once a Paladin becomes immune to the Frightened condition at tenth level, that makes Fearless a dead racial feature"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rangers need a bit of Wisdom for spellcasting, which can make Taunt viable.\n\tHowever, they often need their Bonus Action for things like spells, commanding\n\tpets, two-weapon fighting, and using damage boosts provided by many ranger\n\tsubclasses, so there is a lot of competition for your Bonus Action."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rogues have too much competition for their Bonus Action to fit Taunt into\n\ttheir tactics easily."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "While Bonus Action spells exist, they are in the minority, so there will be many turns where your Bonus Action is free to use on Taunt. While drawing attention to yourself may feel counterproductive as a caster, if you’re fighting from a safe distance, it’s a great way to protect your party’s front line with minimal consequences."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "While Bonus Action spells exist, they are in the minority, so there will be many turns where your Bonus Action is free to use on Taunt. While drawing attention to yourself may feel counterproductive as a caster, if you’re fighting from a safe distance (relatively easy for someone using both Repelling Blast and Lance of Lethargy), it’s a great way to protect your party’s front line with minimal consequences."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "While Bonus Action spells exist, they are in the minority, so there will be many turns where your Bonus Action is free to use on Taunt. While drawing attention to yourself may feel counterproductive as a caster, if you’re fighting from a safe distance (or happen to be an exceptionally sturdy Wizard like "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "War",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/classes/wizard/subclasses/war-magic/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Abjuration",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/classes/wizard/subclasses/abjuration/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "), it’s a great way to protect your party’s front line with minimal consequences."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
